  "response": "여기 스스로 오신 게 아니라 인사팀 권유로 오신 거, 그 부분 먼저 짚고 갈게요. 원치 않는 자리에 앉아 있으면 말문이 막히는 게 당연한 거니까, 오늘 여기서 뭘 해야 할지 모르겠다는 느낌은 충분히 이해합니다.\n\n그리고 팀장으로서 3개월 동안 같은 문제가 반복되면 답답하고, 결국 강하게 말하게 되는 심정도 충분히 짐작이 갑니다.\n\n다만 하나 여쭤보고 싶은 게 있어요. 지금 이 상황에서 본인이 가장 불쾌한 지점은 어디예요? 그 팀원이 실적을 올리지 못한 것 자체인가요, 아니면 그 일이 폭언으로 신고가 들어간 건가요?",
  "reasoning": "The person has come to counseling not by their own choice but mandated by HR. They're expressing frustration about being reported for verbal abuse toward a team member, while also acknowledging they spoke harshly. They're defending their actions as necessary as a team leader.\n\nKey things to address:\n1. They didn't choose to come here - acknowledge this without being dismissive\n2. They're feeling unfairly treated (\"너무하지 않나\") - validate the feeling of frustration without validating the behavior\n3. They did acknowledge \"세게 말한 건 맞습니다\" - there's some self-awareness there\n4. They're framing it as a performance issue and their responsibility as team leader\n\nI should:\n- Acknowledge the involuntary nature of being here without being preachy\n- Not dismiss their frustration about being reported\n- Not validate the behavior either - they're asking for validation that it was \"too much\" to be reported\n- Gently hold space for both: their frustration at the situation AND the fact that something happened that was reported\n- Maybe explore what they actually want from this situation, since they're here involuntarily\n\nI'll keep it concise, natural, and not overly warm or clinical. They seem somewhat guarded and somewhat angry/defensive. I should match their directness somewhat while not being cold.\n",
